course: dictionary
lesson: 14289
title: Notify: telling someone officially
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To notify someone means to tell them about something in an official way, often in writing. «Please notify the school if your child will be away.» Notify is a little more formal than tell. You notify a school, the police or a teacher, usually because they need to know something important.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «If you see something dangerous, notify a teacher straight away.» The words straight away tell you the news is urgent, and the sentence says a grown-up must hear it. Notify means to tell, but the telling is serious and official.
explain: You will also see notify in set phrases. «The council will notify residents of the new bin days.» Here notify someone of something means to inform people about something. When someone is told, we say they were notified, and the message itself is a notification.
fact: Notify means to officially tell someone about something, often in writing.
fact: Notify is a verb: notify, notifies, notified, notifying.
fact: You can notify someone of something: «The council will notify residents of the change.»
fact: A notification is a message that tells you something, such as «a notification on your phone».
fact: Apps notify you when something happens, like when a new message arrives.
exercise: «If you see something dangerous, notify a teacher.» What does notify mean here? | Ignore | Dance with | *Officially tell | Hide from
exercise: Which word is the noun form of notify? | notified | notifying | *notification | notifies
exercise: «Please notify the school if your child will be absent.» Who should be told? | The moon | *The school | The shop on the corner | Nobody, it is only a game
test: Which sentence uses notify correctly? | The cat notified the sofa of its nap. | The dog notifies happily under the table. | I notify my lunch because I am hungry. | *The council will notify residents of the new bin days.
test: Which word means nearly the same as notify in a formal voice? | sleep | giggle | *inform | dash
exam: «Parents were notified by letter that the trip was cancelled.» What does notified mean? | *Officially told | Given a prize | Taken away | Forgotten
